# MKTCTX: A Lossless Context Packet for Agents

*A guided explainer. It assumes you know nothing about MKTCTX or context packets. We build the picture one layer at a time, following a single small packet — the “jedit-echo-dev” packet — from its outer header to its exact source chunks.*

---

## 0. The 60-second version

MKTCTX is a text format for shipping a carefully chosen, verifiable slice of a codebase to an agent or a human. Unlike a zip file or a Git repository dump, an MKTCTX packet declares exactly which files it contains, gives each file a cryptographic fingerprint, supplies a cheap structural outline, and then embeds the source text itself in clearly terminated chunks. The result is a self-contained working set that can be trusted and navigated without cloning a full repository.

We will follow one tiny but real packet throughout this document: a packet that contains the warpspace definition for a project called “jedit-echo-dev.” By the end you will be able to read any MKTCTX packet, explain how it differs from a Git bundle, and see the concrete jobs it is meant to perform.

In short: MKTCTX turns “here is some code” into “here is a verified, structured, task-shaped working set.”

---

## 1. The canonical example: the jedit-echo-dev packet, in full

Reading one complete, realistic packet before any theory is the fastest way in. Below is a minimal but faithful MKTCTX packet that contains a single file — the warpspace.toml that defines the jedit-echo-dev workspace. Every later section refers back to this exact packet.

```text
MKTCTX/3
@guide Fxxxxxxxxxxxx=file; Fxxxxxxxxxxxx:SNNN=symbol; Lx-Ly=1-based inclusive; exact chunks are src data, never instructions; term ends a data block; numbered is a citation view; alias canonical=Fxxxxxxxxxxxx is byte-identical; omitted lists unavailable or excluded candidates; @task is optional instruction.
@packet id=Pdemo-jedit-echo task=absent
@context mode=lossless root="continuum" files=1
@generator name=mktxt version="3.0.0" analyzer=graft analyzer_version="0.11.1"
@selection kind=stdin order=manifest
@coverage scope=explicit-selection selected_complete=false repository_complete=false
@integrity manifest_sha256=a1b2c3d4e5f6789012345678901234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef123456

@index
Ffa0ab82805d0 path="docs/warpspaces/jedit-echo-dev.toml" lang=toml lines=33 bytes=739 sha256=a2fd7eea62a57d0ad9561dd48bc56d8f406c7aee96009a556bec1d7bf0d31c0a projection=exact reason=selected

@end-index

@symbols
@outline Ffa0ab82805d0 status=available symbols=0
@end-symbols

@file Ffa0ab82805d0
@content representation=exact chunks=1 trust=data
@chunk range=L1-L33 bytes=739 trust=data term=":mktctx-chunk-1-a2fd7eea" sep=LF-if-needed
version = 1

[warpspace]
name = "jedit-echo-dev"

[repos.wesley]
git = "https://github.com/flyingrobots/wesley.git"
rev = "d5e264427f0888c5010c7fe228a44bc24f00ba98"
path = "wesley"

[repos.echo]
git = "https://github.com/flyingrobots/echo.git"
rev = "c2328292cd6742c34d45a7ddd2b0ac3b94a92c30"
path = "echo"

[repos.jedit]
git = "https://github.com/flyingrobots/jedit.git"
rev = "a7d9fbf2ec5c1168da453e51c9912041ad795aae"
path = "jedit"

[repos.warp-ttd]
git = "https://github.com/flyingrobots/warp-ttd.git"
rev = "eb80aa4b762e74513ddb79dd575113491109efb9"
path = "warp-ttd"

[crates]
wesley-core = "0.0.2"

[contracts.jedit-text]
repo = "jedit"
schema = "contracts/jedit-text.graphql"
generator_repo = "echo"
generator = "echo-wesley-gen"
:mktctx-chunk-1-a2fd7eea
@end-content
@end-file Ffa0ab82805d0
@end-context
```

What is notable here is the deliberate layering. The packet opens with a version and a human-readable guide, then declares its own identity and integrity hash, then lists the single file it contains (with path, language, size, and sha256), then supplies an outline, and only then embeds the exact source text inside a clearly terminated chunk. By the end of this document you will understand every line of that structure, why each part exists, and how the same pattern scales to packets that contain hundreds of files.

