## 2. Background: the cast and the mental model

An MKTCTX packet is not a repository and not an archive in the ordinary sense. It is a self-describing message that answers four questions at once: what was selected, why it was selected, what the selected material looks like structurally, and what the exact bytes of that material are.

The main players inside every packet are:

- the **packet header** (version, guide, identity, integrity),
- the **index** (one row per selected file),
- the **symbol outlines** (cheap structural summaries),
- the **file content blocks** (exact source, chunked and terminated).

These pieces always appear in that order. A consumer can stop after the index, or after the outlines, or continue all the way to the source text.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Packet arrives] --> B[Read header<br/>version + guide + integrity]
    B --> C[Read index<br/>files + paths + sha256s]
    C --> D{Need structure?}
    D -- yes --> E[Read symbol outlines]
    D -- no --> F{Need source?}
    E --> F
    F -- yes --> G[Read exact content chunks]
    F -- no --> H[Done — enough context]
    G --> H
```

<details>
<summary>Figure 1 - How a consumer walks an MKTCTX packet</summary>

Figure 1: A consumer can stop at any layer. The header and index are always present; outlines and exact content are loaded only when needed.
</details>

| Layer | What it gives you | Typical cost |
|---|---|---|
| Header | Version, packet id, overall integrity hash | Tiny |
| Index | Path, language, size, per-file sha256, selection reason | Small |
| Symbol outlines | Functions, types, headings, line ranges | Medium |
| Exact content | Byte-identical source text in terminated chunks | Large |

> **Intuition to carry forward:** MKTCTX is designed so that an agent can decide *how much* of the packet to load. The expensive exact text is never forced on the reader.

In summary, the format is a progressive-disclosure envelope: metadata first, structure second, exact source last.

