## 3. What already existed: Git bundles and ordinary context shipping

Before MKTCTX, the common ways to hand a body of code to another person or process were Git clones, zip archives, and Git bundles. A Git bundle is the closest relative, so it is worth understanding clearly.

A Git bundle is a single file that contains a pack of Git objects (commits, trees, blobs) plus a short list of the refs it makes available. You create one with a command such as:

```bash
git bundle create repo.bundle main v1.2.3
```

The resulting file can be cloned or fetched from later, even offline. It preserves history, authorship, and the ability to run ordinary Git commands. What it does *not* do is tell a consumer which files matter for a particular task, supply structural outlines, or guarantee that the text an agent sees is the exact text that was selected.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Git repository] --> B[git bundle create]
    B --> C[Binary packfile + ref list]
    C --> D[git clone / git fetch]
    D --> E[Working tree + full history]
    style C fill:#d1ecf1,stroke:#3498db,color:#000
```

<details>
<summary>Figure 2 - The Git-bundle path</summary>

Figure 2: A Git bundle moves objects and refs. The consumer still has to decide what to read and must trust the resulting working tree.
</details>

| Situation | Git bundle outcome |
|---|---|
| Need history and the ability to commit | Excellent |
| Need a task-shaped slice of source | Must still select files after unpacking |
| Need cryptographic proof of the exact text an agent saw | Possible, but not first-class |
| Need a human- or agent-readable structural outline | Absent |

In summary, Git bundles solve the problem of moving a repository. They leave the problems of selection, navigation, and agent-friendly presentation to the consumer.

