## 4. The gap that MKTCTX fills, shown on the jedit-echo-dev packet

The gap appears the moment the consumer is an agent (or a human doing focused analysis) rather than a developer who wants a full working tree. Consider the jedit-echo-dev packet again. An agent that receives only a Git bundle of the continuum repository still has to:

1. materialize a working tree,
2. discover which files are relevant to warpspaces,
3. open those files,
4. hope nothing was altered in transit.

An agent that receives the MKTCTX packet can:

1. read the index and immediately see the single selected file and its sha256,
2. optionally read the (empty) outline,
3. load the exact terminated chunk,
4. verify the content against the declared hash.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant A as Agent
    participant P as MKTCTX packet
    A->>P: Read header + integrity
    P-->>A: Packet id + manifest_sha256
    A->>P: Read index
    P-->>A: Ffa0ab82805d0 + path + sha256
    A->>P: Request exact chunk
    P-->>A: Terminated source text
    Note over A: Verify sha256 matches
```

<details>
<summary>Figure 3 - Agent interaction with the jedit-echo-dev packet</summary>

Figure 3: The agent never needs a working tree. Selection, integrity, and exact text are all inside the packet.
</details>

| Need | Git bundle | MKTCTX (jedit-echo-dev) |
|---|---|---|
| Know which file was chosen | No | Yes — explicit index row |
| Know why it was chosen | No | Yes — `reason=selected` |
| Verify exact bytes | Indirect | Direct per-file + packet sha256 |
| Get a structural outline first | No | Yes (even if empty) |
| Avoid materializing a repo | No | Yes |

In summary, MKTCTX does not replace Git bundles; it answers a different question. The bundle answers “how do I obtain the repository?” The packet answers “what should I actually look at, and can I trust this view?”

