## 5. How an MKTCTX packet is built

A packet is produced by a generator (in the real continuum tree the generator is called `mktxt`). The generator is given a selection of files — either an explicit list or the result of a policy — and emits the layers in order.

The load-bearing steps are:

1. Compute the per-file sha256 of the exact source.
2. Emit the index row that records path, language, size, hash, and selection reason.
3. Optionally compute a symbol outline (functions, types, headings).
4. Emit the source as one or more terminated chunks.
5. Compute the overall manifest_sha256 over the structured packet.

For the jedit-echo-dev example the generator produced a single chunk because the file is only 739 bytes. Larger files are split; each chunk still carries its own terminator so a truncated or concatenated packet is detectable.

```text
@chunk range=L1-L33 bytes=739 trust=data term=":mktctx-chunk-1-a2fd7eea" sep=LF-if-needed
…exact source…
:mktctx-chunk-1-a2fd7eea
```

The terminator string embeds a short hash of the chunk content. A consumer that does not see the matching terminator knows the chunk is incomplete or has been tampered with.

In summary, the build process turns a selection of files into a layered, hash-addressed message whose every claim can be checked.

---

## 6. How a packet is verified

Verification is deliberately cheap and local. No network and no Git installation are required.

1. Parse the header and confirm the MKTCTX version is understood.
2. Recompute the manifest_sha256 over the packet body and compare it with the declared value.
3. For every index row that is actually used, re-hash the corresponding exact content and compare it with the sha256 stored in the index.
4. Confirm that every content chunk ends with its declared terminator.

If any of those checks fail, the packet is rejected. The jedit-echo-dev packet declares both a packet-level hash and the file-level hash `a2fd7eea62a57d0ad9561dd48bc56d8f406c7aee96009a556bec1d7bf0d31c0a`. A consumer can recompute either or both.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Receive packet] --> B{manifest_sha256 matches?}
    B -- no --> X[Reject]
    B -- yes --> C{For each used file}
    C --> D{content sha256 matches?}
    D -- no --> X
    D -- yes --> E{chunk terminators present?}
    E -- no --> X
    E -- yes --> F[Accept]
    style X fill:#f8d7da,stroke:#c0392b,color:#000
    style F fill:#d4edda,stroke:#27ae60,color:#000
```

<details>
<summary>Figure 4 - Verification pipeline</summary>

Figure 4: Three independent checks — packet hash, per-file hashes, and chunk terminators — must all pass.
</details>

| Check | What it detects |
|---|---|
| manifest_sha256 | Any alteration of the packet structure or content |
| per-file sha256 | Substitution or corruption of a single source file |
| chunk terminator | Truncation or splicing of a content block |

In summary, verification turns the integrity fields that look decorative into a practical gate: either the packet is exactly what the generator emitted, or it is discarded.

