## 7. Potential use cases

Because the format is both exact and navigable, it fits several recurring jobs.

**Agent cold-start.**
Instead of “clone the monorepo and look around,” an operator ships a packet that already contains the design docs, contract schemas, and core packages relevant to the task. The agent begins with a verified working set rather than a search problem.

**Multi-agent hand-off.**
One agent produces or refreshes a packet; the next agent receives that packet as its sole source of truth. The packet id and manifest hash become a stable reference that can be logged or cited.

**Design-and-implementation loops.**
In a system such as Continuum, design packets, GraphQL contract families, and runtime packages travel together. An MKTCTX packet can freeze a coherent cross-section so that work on one piece is performed against a known view of the others.

**Review and audit.**
Because every chunk is exact and range-addressable, a reviewer can cite `Ffa0ab82805d0:L12-L18` with confidence that the cited text is the text that was examined.

**Onboarding and curriculum packets.**
Curated, versioned packets can be published for “understand the warpspace model” or “understand the contract-family registry.” New contributors receive a high-signal slice instead of the entire tree.

**Complement to Git bundles.**
A release process can emit both a Git bundle (for archival and full-repo reconstruction) and an MKTCTX packet (for agent and reviewer consumption). The two artifacts answer different questions about the same commit.

In each of these cases the jedit-echo-dev packet illustrates the pattern at small scale: a single, purposeful file, fully described, fully hashed, and ready for an agent to load without further discovery.

